<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> International English</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Communication Skills Are Important in the Workplace?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the linear structure of a short formal talk. Interactive: Facilitate discussions on future career paths.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow key points in a structured talk about career planning (e.g., Explain the speaker's advice on gaining experience, for example, "He said that we should gain more experience in our field by taking on diverse projects or internships.")
Can facilitate discussions on career options, prompting classmates to share ideas on communication-related skills (e.g., "Which industries in Panama require English fluency, and why?")
Can ask follow-up questions to encourage classmates to elaborate on career choices (e.g., "Can you explain which skills can help you advance your career?")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher plays short audio snippets (e.g., a phone call in an office, someone giving a presentation, two colleagues discussing a project) and asks students to identify the type of workplace communication. Students listen and raise their hands to guess. Teacher asks, "What do you hear? How important are these sounds in a workplace?" This activates prior knowledge about `workplaces` and `communication` `skills` and sets the stage for listening.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary words related to the theme: `fluency`, `cross-cultural communication`, `global interactions`, `relevant`, `necessary`. The teacher pronounces each word clearly, emphasizing the long vowel sound /e/ in `relevant` and `necessary`, and modeling the alternation of full and reduced vowels in `communication` /kəˌmjuː.nɪˈkeɪ.ʃən/. The teacher provides a brief definition and an example sentence for each. (e.g., "Fluency means being able to speak a language smoothly and effectively. English fluency is often necessary in international business.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone has English `fluency`, can they only understand a little, or can they speak it well?" (Expected answer: "They can speak it well.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does `cross-cultural communication` mean? Is it talking to people from the same `backgrounds` or different `backgrounds`?" (Expected answer: "Talking to people from different backgrounds.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International English".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of a short formal talk/podcast segment (approx. 2-3 minutes) titled "The Role of English and Essential Communication Skills in Global Business." The genre is a formal informative talk, with a formal register, suitable for `international English` `business` `contexts`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the linear structure of a short formal talk.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen once. Teacher asks: "What is the main topic of the speaker's talk?" and "Why has `English` `become` such an `important` language for `international` `business`?" (Pragmatic: Asking about English's global role). Students share their initial understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (Detailed):</b> Students listen again while completing a True/False exercise or matching statements to key pieces of advice from the talk. The statements will include target grammar and vocabulary, e.g., "The speaker mentioned that `cross-cultural communication` `skills` `are` `vital` for `global` `interactions`." (True/False). "He said that we `should` `gain` more `experience` through `internships`." (True/False, using modals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Check:</b> After the second listening, students are asked to identify two pieces of advice from the talk that they find `relevant` or `necessary` for their future `workplaces`. This allows them to listen for specific `resources` or `skills`.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific information; using Present Perfect, Modals (must, could, should, can), and Indirect Questions; target vocabulary: `English`, `workplaces`, `communication`, `skills`, `fluency`, `resources`, `business`, `diplomacy`, `lingua franca`, `backgrounds`, `employees`, `courses`, `access`, `contexts`, `cross-cultural communication`, `online platforms`, `academic resources`, `global`, `interactions`, `spread`, `learn`, `practice`, `connect`, `recognize`, `become`, `accessible`, `relevant`, `necessary`, `important`, `formal`, `fluent`, `communicative`, `proficient`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening Cloze):</b> The teacher plays selected sentences from the formal talk, pausing at key vocabulary words or grammatical structures (e.g., Present Perfect verbs, modals) that have been intentionally omitted. Students listen and fill in the blanks on a handout. Example: "English has _______ a global `lingua franca` for `business` `interactions`." (become). "You `must` develop strong `communicative` `skills`." Students also pay attention to the pronunciation of `/e/` sounds and reduced vowels as they listen and write.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Rephrasing Advice):</b> In pairs, students receive a list of incomplete sentences based on the speaker's advice from the audio. They `must` rephrase these using modals (`should`, `could`, `can`) or indirect questions, actively listening to their partner's suggestions. Example prompt: "The speaker said it's good to gain experience." Student A: "He suggested that we `could` `gain` `experience` through `internships`." Student B: "Can you explain which `skills` `can` `help` you `advance` your career?" (Using indirect questions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check & Pronunciation Review:</b> Students compare their filled-in cloze exercises and rephrased sentences with another pair. They listen carefully to each other's responses, correcting any discrepancies. The teacher circulates, offering feedback on the correct usage of vocabulary and grammar, and gently correcting pronunciation of words like `accessible` /əkˈses.ə.bəl/ and `relevant`.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International English".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Collaborative Career Path Discussion</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 `students`). Their task is to discuss and identify at least three `workplaces` or industries in Panama that currently require strong `English` `fluency` and `cross-cultural communication` `skills`, linking their ideas to the advice from the formal talk. One `student` in each group `must` act as a facilitator, actively listening to their group members' contributions and asking `indirect questions` and `follow-up questions` to encourage elaboration. For example, "Which `industries` in Panama `require` `English` `fluency`, and why?" or "Can you `explain` `which` `skills` `can` `help` you `advance` your career in that field?" (Sociolinguistic: Using formal language in workplace contexts; Pragmatic: Discussing communication skills). Students `must` use `formal` language appropriate for discussing career development, and integrate target vocabulary and grammatical structures from the lesson (e.g., `Present Perfect`, `modals`).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The facilitator ensures all group members `engage` and `express` their ideas clearly, `recognizing` the need to `adapt` language for `different` `audiences` within the group. Other group members listen attentively to their peers' ideas before responding or building upon them.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment: Observation Checklist</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher uses an observation checklist during the group discussions, noting student participation, ability to follow the talk's key points, `fluency` in facilitating/asking `follow-up questions`, and the appropriate use of target grammar (`Present Perfect`, `modals`, `indirect questions`) and vocabulary (`workplaces`, `communication`, `skills`, `fluency`, `cross-cultural communication`, `international`, `global`).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups present a brief summary (1-2 minutes) of their discussion's conclusions to the class, outlining the industries they identified and the key communication skills required. Students listen to other groups' summaries and compare ideas.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /e/ sound and reduced vowels).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., `fluency`, `cross-cultural communication`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining key points from the formal talk.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective facilitation and asking of `indirect questions`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List two new `communication` `skills` or ideas about `workplaces` you `have` `learned` today from the talk or your group discussion."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident `are` you now in following a `formal` talk about career planning? (1 = Not confident, 5 = Very confident)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about listening to the formal talk, and what helped you understand it better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How `can` you `practice` your `listening` `skills` outside of class to better understand `international` `English` `contexts`?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The `listening` tasks were well-received, and `students` demonstrated good comprehension of the main ideas. The modeling of pronunciation for `relevant` and `communication` was effective, as `students` showed awareness during practice. The group discussion provided a good opportunity for `students` to use `indirect questions` and `modals` for giving advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some `students` still need more encouragement to `access` and `employ` specific vocabulary like `diplomacy` or `lingua franca` in their spoken output. For the next lesson, I `should` provide more explicit scaffolding for using these words in written sentences. I also need to reinforce the difference between `formal` and informal language more systematically for certain `contexts`.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, `students` will read an article using the same `vocabulary` words and grammatical structures, focusing on reading comprehension to reinforce contextual understanding of `communication skills` in various `workplaces` and `global` settings, building on their `listening` foundation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>